//? Title: Bayesian bootstrap for a mean and median
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

> Draw a posterior distribution for each statistic

Each Bayesian bootstrap draw assigns the observed rows a random vector of
probability weights from a Dirichlet distribution with all parameters equal
to one. Repeated values have separate row weights that add at the same
point in the empirical distribution. The code evaluates two functionals of
that distribution, the weighted mean and the inverse-CDF median.

```{cpp, id=bayesian-bootstrap, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/stats/bootstrap_designs.hpp>
#include <datamunge/stats/robust_summary.hpp>
#include <numeric>
#include <print>

int main() {
    using namespace datamunge::stats;
    const std::vector<double> sample{2, 3, 3, 8, 13};
    constexpr std::size_t draws = 12000;
    datamunge::random::SplitMix64 mean_rng(42), median_rng(42);

    const auto means = bayesian_bootstrap(sample.size(), draws, [&](const auto& weights) {
        return std::inner_product(weights.begin(), weights.end(), sample.begin(), 0.0);
    }, mean_rng);
    const auto medians = bayesian_bootstrap(sample.size(), draws, [&](const auto& weights) {
        return weighted_empirical_quantiles(sample, weights, {.5}).front();
    }, median_rng);

    std::println("| Statistic | Posterior average | 2.5th percentile | 97.5th percentile |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    const auto summarize = [](const char* statistic, const std::vector<double>& posterior) {
        const auto limits = sample_quantiles(posterior, {.025, .975}, 1);
        const double average = std::accumulate(posterior.begin(), posterior.end(), 0.0)
                             / static_cast<double>(posterior.size());
        std::println("| {} | {:.2f} | {:.2f} | {:.2f} |", statistic, average, limits[0], limits[1]);
    };
    summarize("Mean", means);
    summarize("Median", medians);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Statistic | Posterior average | 2.5th percentile | 97.5th percentile |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Mean | 5.80 | 3.13 | 9.58 |
| Median | 4.83 | 2.00 | 13.00 |
// result_end

The generators start from the same seed, so both functionals see the same
weight vectors. The percentiles estimate equal-tailed posterior bounds.
The posterior median draws are discrete and can only take observed values.

[Rubin (1981), The Bayesian Bootstrap|https://doi.org/10.1214/aos/1176345338].
